result.summary:
I read all three engines in full: the v2 legacy copy (2448 lines), the early engine (2548 lines) and Liquidity Flow Pro (1615 lines), plus their READMEs and the LFP simulator. The v2 legacy copy is byte-identical to the frozen baseline, so v2 line numbers are valid for both. I also read HANDOFF, the 49 overhaul findings, the APEX spec, the reference screenshot and twelve study reports.

v2 is the right base. Its probability maths is verified correct by the study, and its non-repaint MTF pattern, exact anchoring, external registry and distance-matched ledger are all sound. Its scoring and selection layer is mostly unsupported or wrong-signed:
- the draw vector D has no skill (47-54% across 11 markets);
- the raid-to-D mapping is reversed;
- the volume-profile path cost is wrong;
- f_calMult is noise;
- the EDGE verdict peeks;
- the ETA, heat and EV readouts are biased by construction.

The early engine is the only one with outcome tests (README claims; its scripts are not in the repo, so I could not re-run them). Every test rejected liquidity type, bias, sweeps and the score as predictors. The DOL side was taken first 59.3% of the time, equal to distance alone. Sweep Reversal lost (1,350 trades, 38% win, -0.06R per trade). The later study replicated all of this independently.

LFP is a pool-share map, not a DOL engine. I re-ran its sim: 15/15 behavioural checks pass. Its UP/DOWN percentage has no predictive test: on driftless random walks it flips side about every 68 bars and reads EXTREME on 7.35% of bars. Its lifecycle primitives are the best of the three.

No single engine supplies the strongest liquidity and target logic; APEX should combine them:
- v2: sources, registry, maths, empirical surface, ledger;
- LFP: sweep/taken/test lifecycle, bounded clustering, partial-period discard, volume quality, LOW DATA;
- early engine: PMH/PML, auto timeframe ladder, time-anchored drawings, volatility clock, Brier scorecard, optional lock-until-hit, cost-aware tradeability.

Geometry (distance, volatility, clock) is the selection backbone. ICT features start neutral and must earn weight per chart behind a gate.

Mapped against spec sections 3-18:
- strong: section 4 (liquidity) and section 10 (statistics);
- partial: sections 3, 5, 11, 15, 16, 17 and 18;
- missing: section 4.8 (destination vs reversal), sections 6, 7, 8 and 9 (no regime classifier with hysteresis), section 12, section 13 (signal states) and section 14 (forward path).

The 49 overhaul findings reduce to about 35 distinct defects; only 3 are verified. The study found about 12 more that the audit missed.

Compile status of all three files on TradingView is unverified. Dead code: v2 has f_changed (865) and nine destructured MTF values that are never read (923-925); the early engine has f_flagW (490) and the unused exec-TF tuple (922); LFP has adxVal (885).
